{%- comment -%}
  The inline declarations a text style resolves to.

  **One place decides what a style means**, shared by every block that offers
  one. `product-card-text` and `product-card-option-values` each carried the
  same ladder — font role, size, letter case, colour, five `case` statements
  apiece — and an audit put the duplication at roughly 5,100 identical
  characters across the two files. A third block offering text would have made
  it three copies.

  **It prints inline declarations rather than a class name, and that is
  deliberate.** `.text-caption` and `.card__meta` are both one class of
  specificity, and `.card__meta` is declared later in `base.css`, so a class
  would lose to the very rule it is supposed to override — silently, with the
  markup looking exactly right. Inline wins outright and needs no ordering
  contract between two distant parts of the stylesheet.

  **An untouched style prints nothing at all**, which is what makes the control
  additive: the block keeps whatever `.card__meta` gives it until a merchant
  chooses otherwise. That is the rule this theme is built to — the fallback
  lives in the CSS, so adding a control changes nothing until someone uses it.

  **A style says nothing about the element.** Whether a thing is a heading is a
  question about the page's outline, and every caller asks it separately.

  Accepts:
    style - a style key, or 'inherit' / blank for none.
    color - a colour, or blank to inherit.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign known = 'display,title,subtitle,lead,body,small,caption' | split: ','
  assign out = ''

  comment
    Every property comes from the style's own token, so the seven definitions
    stay in `theme-tokens.liquid` and an unrecognised stored value fails as an
    unresolved token rather than as a wrong number. The guard is what keeps that
    true: without it a stale key would print `var(--text-foo-size)`, which is
    invalid at computed-value time and would take the declaration with it.
  endcomment
  if known contains style
    assign out = out | append: 'font-family: var(--text-' | append: style | append: '-font);'
    assign out = out | append: 'font-size: var(--text-' | append: style | append: '-size);'
    assign out = out | append: 'line-height: var(--text-' | append: style | append: '-leading);'
    assign out = out | append: 'letter-spacing: var(--text-' | append: style | append: '-tracking);'
    assign out = out | append: 'text-transform: var(--text-' | append: style | append: '-case);'
  endif

  comment
    **One colour picker, and empty means inherit.** It was a select of
    inherit / muted / faint / custom with a picker behind the last — four
    options and a conditional field to say one thing. A picker that is empty
    already says "leave it alone", so the select was ceremony.

    A picked colour is absolute and does not follow a scheme, which is the cost
    of the simpler control: a shop using this on both a light and a dark scheme
    has to pick one that works on both, or leave it empty and let the scheme's
    own text colour through.
  endcomment
  if color != blank
    assign out = out | append: 'color: ' | append: color | append: ';'
  endif
-%}
{{- out -}}
